lesson g2-taking-away-with-an-exchange v=1 format=worked grade=2 unit=3 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Taking away with an exchange"
  goal "Take away two-digit numbers in columns, exchanging a ten for ten ones when you need to."

  example {
    worked columns.sub-two-digit a=52 b=27 easy-level=medium hard-level=medium
    say """
      There are not enough ones, so one of the five tens is exchanged for ten ones. Now there are
      twelve ones to take seven from, and four tens left.
      """
  }
  exercises {
    practice sub.exchange-or-not count=2 seed=3
    practice columns.sub-two-digit count=2 seed=6
    practice sub.from-hundred count=2 seed=8
    practice sub.road-further count=2 seed=2
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show sub.smaller-from-bigger ta=7 oa=3 tb=2 ob=8 easy-ta=6 easy-oa=2 easy-ob=6 hard-ta=9 hard-oa=1 hard-tb=4 hard-ob=9
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show sub.no-exchange-count ta=4 oa=3 easy-ta=3 easy-oa=2 hard-ta=6 hard-oa=5
  }

  grown-ups """
    Do the first few with real rods and ones, swapping a rod for ten cubes before writing anything
    down. The writing makes sense once the swap has been done by hand. The first two questions ask
    only whether an exchange is needed, which is the decision the method rests on. A hundred take
    away a number can be done in columns or by counting up from the smaller number; both are fine.
    The two-star question names the commonest mistake, taking the smaller digit from the bigger in
    every column; ask the child why it does not work. In the three-star question a list organised
    by tens digit makes the count manageable.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the numbers stay under sixty, the ones digits are far apart so the exchange is plain, taking from a hundred leaves a small answer, and nine numbers need no exchange in the last question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the numbers reach the eighties, the ones digits are close so the exchange has to be checked, taking from a hundred leaves seventy or more, and thirty-six numbers need no exchange in the last question."
  }
}
